            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: Tiering Is About Resource Allocation</h2>
<p>The biggest waste for multi-account ad teams isn't budget that won't spend—it's team energy evenly distributed across accounts of varying value. The core goal of account tiering is simple: high-value accounts get more attention, low-value accounts don't consume excessive resources. In practice, we see many teams with 5+ accounts using a "rotation" approach—30 minutes per account daily—which means no account ever reaches its optimal performance. Tiered management solves the first problem: <strong>stop replacing resource allocation with time distribution</strong>. </p>
<h2>Three Dimensions for Determining Account Tiers</h2>
<p>How should accounts be tiered? We recommend evaluating across three dimensions:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Budget Scale</strong>: A monthly spend of ¥100,000 and ¥10,000 clearly require different management depth</p>
<p><strong>Risk Exposure</strong>: Accounts involving new product testing or aggressive scaling carry higher risk</p>
<p><strong>Team Roles</strong>: Whoever is accountable for account results should invest more time</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Tier 1: Core Accounts (15%-20% of total)</h3>
<p>These typically account for 60%-70% of total spend. Take a utility app as an example: their primary market Google Ads account exceeds ¥800,000 monthly, and ROAS fluctuations directly impact overall performance. Key management points:<br />
- Deep inspection at least twice daily, not just checking dashboards<br />
- Dedicated senior optimization specialist<br />
- Automated rules + manual review as dual insurance<br />
- Lower balance warning thresholds than other tiers</p>
<h3>Tier 2: Growth Accounts (40%-50% of total)</h3>
<p>These carry out scaling and testing tasks, with budgets typically 30%-50% of core accounts. A cross-border e-commerce team has 3 TikTok accounts dedicated to creative testing—these don't need meticulous management but require rapid validation of creative direction. Key management points:<br />
- Batch operations prioritized, reducing manual adjustments per account<br />
- Creative testing cycle: 48-72 hours for conclusions<br />
- Pause for investigation if performance drops below baseline for 3 consecutive days</p>
<h3>Tier 3: Long-tail Accounts (30%-40% of total)</h3>
<p>Low individual spend but numerous accounts with dispersed geographies. Same brand's European small-language-market accounts each spend less than ¥20,000 monthly, but combined contribute significant incremental volume. Key management points:<br />
- Maximize automated rules, minimize manual intervention<br />
- Unified templated campaign structures<br />
- Batch export prioritized for financial reconciliation</p>
<h2>After Tiering: Supporting Mechanisms Determine Execution</h2>
<p>Creating a tiering plan is easy; implementing it is hard. These supporting mechanisms determine whether tiering truly lands:</p>
<p><strong>1. Tiered Balance Warning Settings</strong><br />
Different tiers have different impacts when balance runs out. A core account running dry—even for hours—can miss critical conversion opportunities. Recommend: core accounts warn when balance falls below 3x daily spend; growth accounts at 2x; long-tail accounts 24 hours in advance based on weekly spend projections.</p>
<p><strong>2. Report Aggregation Permission Design</strong><br />
In multi-account management, data aggregation consumes the most time. Set different report permissions for different roles: team leads see global aggregate reports, optimizers see detailed reports for assigned accounts, finance sees spend and balance summaries only. This improves efficiency while controlling information leakage.</p>
<p><strong>3. Regular Reviews and Tier Adjustments</strong><br />
Account tiers aren't static. A growth account that performs exceptionally one month may become a core account the next; a core account that's been stable for two consecutive quarters can be appropriately downgraded to free up team capacity. Recommend quarterly tier reviews, dynamically adjusting based on performance data.</p>
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>Account tiering的本质，是承认团队精力是稀缺资源。当我们不再试图用“公平”的方式分配注意力，而是根据账户的真实价值分配资源，投放效率的提升会是自然结果。</p>
<p>The essence of account tiering is acknowledging that team energy is a scarce resource. When we stop trying to distribute attention "fairly" and instead allocate resources based on true account value, improved ad efficiency becomes the natural result.</p>
<p>Tiering isn't the goal—optimizing resource allocation is. If you're managing 5+ ad accounts, ask yourself: which tier of accounts did I spend the most time on today? Is that choice reasonable?</p>
